# Configuring `links` Parameter

<!-- Using anchor links produces a Velocity error.-->

You can add cross reference links to external projects using the [&lt;links/&gt;](../javadoc-mojo.html) parameter. For instance:

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <reporting> (or <build>)
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-javadoc-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <links>
            <link>https://commons.apache.org/proper/commons-dbcp/apidocs/</link>
            <link>https://commons.apache.org/proper/commons-lang/apidocs/</link>
          </links>
            ...
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </reporting> (or </build>)
  ...
</project>
```

**Important Note**: according the [Javadoc specifications](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/specs/man/javadoc.html#option-link), all given links **should** have a fetchable `/package-list` or `/element-list` file.

You can try to detect all Javadoc links for the project's dependencies. You need to use the [&lt;detectLinks/&gt;](../javadoc-mojo.html#detectLinks) parameter. Detected links are based on the default Maven conventions. For instance, if your project has a dependency on [Apache Commons Lang](https://commons.apache.org/proper/commons-lang/) such as:

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>commons-lang</groupId>
      <artifactId>commons-lang</artifactId>
      <version>2.6</version>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
  ...
</project>
```

The added Javadoc link is the `<url/>` of the dependency's POM followed by `/apidocs`, here `http://commons.apache.org/lang/apidocs`.

A link to the Java SE API documentation is added for the Java version of your project. The plugin takes that version from its [&lt;release/&gt;](../javadoc-mojo.html#release) parameter (the `maven.compiler.release` property), then from its [&lt;source/&gt;](../javadoc-mojo.html#source) parameter (the `maven.compiler.source` property), then from the [&lt;source/&gt;](https://maven.apache.org/plugins/maven-compiler-plugin/compile-mojo.html#source) parameter of the [`org.apache.maven.plugins:maven-compiler-plugin`](https://maven.apache.org/plugins/maven-compiler-plugin/) (defined in ${esc.d}{project.build.plugins} or in ${esc.d}{project.build.pluginManagement}), and finally from the version of the Javadoc Tool executable. For Java 16 and later the plugin adds no link, because the Javadoc tool links to the Java SE API documentation itself. To skip this link, you need to configure [&lt;detectJavaApiLink/&gt;](../javadoc-mojo.html#detectJavaApiLink) to `false`.

**Note**: to link to another copy of the Java SE API documentation, for example an internal mirror, set its URL with the [&lt;javaApiLinks/&gt;](../javadoc-mojo.html#javaApiLinks) parameter. The key is `api_` followed by the Java version (`api_11`; `api_1.8` for Java 8). For example:

```xml
<configuration>
  <javaApiLinks>
    <property>
      <name>api_11</name>
      <value>https://docs.example.com/jdk11/api/</value>
    </property>
  </javaApiLinks>
  ...
</configuration>
```

<!-- Using anchor links produces a Velocity error.-->

Refer to [&lt;links/&gt;](../javadoc-mojo.html) parameter for more information.

# Configuring `offlineLinks` Parameter

If your project has modules, you can add cross reference links to your modules when your goals are not aggregator goals. For instance, if your project has two modules, for example:

```xml
<project>
  ...
  <modules>
    <module>module1</module>
    <module>module2</module>
  </modules>
  ...
</project>
```

The offlineLinks for **module1** will be `/absolute/path/to/`**module2**`/target/site/apidocs` and the offlineLinks for **module2** will be `/absolute/path/to/`**module1**`/target/site/apidocs`.

**Important Note**: all offlinelinks are based on the ${esc.d}{project.url} if present.

You can disable the cross reference for `offlineLinks` using the [&lt;detectOfflineLinks/&gt;](../javadoc-mojo.html#detectOfflineLinks) parameter.

Refer to [&lt;offlineLinks/&gt;](../javadoc-mojo.html#offlineLinks) parameter for more information.
